]]}, {"t": "ub", "lp": -0.0005281770136207342, "top": [["ub", -0.0005281770136207342], ["u", -8.09237289428711]]}, {"t": ")", "lp": -0.0016039949841797352, "top": [[")", -0.0016039949841797352], ["):", -6.865915298461914]]}, {"t": " or", "lp": -0.19325314462184906, "top": [[" or", -0.19325314462184906], ["\n", -1.7474714517593384]]}, {"t": " mod", "lp": -0.05343339592218399, "top": [[" mod", -0.05343339592218399], [" module", -3.399294853210449]]}, {"t": "probe", "lp": -0.0007753993268124759, "top": [["probe", -0.0007753993268124759], ["options", -7.768795967102051]]}, {"t": " config", "lp": -0.4872094690799713, "top": [[" config", -0.4872094690799713], ["\n", -1.6060906648635864]]}, {"t": "\n", "lp": -0.0009608711116015911, "top": [["\n", -0.0009608711116015911], [",", -8.03019905090332]]}, {"t": "options", "lp": -0.7976741790771484, "top": [["options", -0.7976741790771484], ["#", -1.3733234405517578]]}, {"t": " xe", "lp": -0.45196229219436646, "top": [[" xe", -0.45196229219436646], [" c", -1.0177371501922607]]}, {"t": " c", "lp": -4.124726547161117e-05, "top": [[" c", -4.124726547161117e-05], [" s", -11.551006317138672]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nCCS stands for **Compute and Copy Engine Scheduler**. These modes control how the GPU's compute and copy engines are scheduled, which matters for multi-GPU setups and workloads.\n\n## The Modes\n\n**CCS Mode 0 (Auto)**\n- The driver automatically selects the appropriate scheduling mode\n- Generally the safe default for most users\n\n**CCS Mode 1**\n- Uses a specific scheduling policy for the compute/copy engines\n- Tends to favor throughput-oriented workloads\n\n**CCS Mode 2**\n- Different scheduling behavior, often tuned differently for compute vs copy operations\n- May behave better for certain mixed workloads\n\n**CCS Mode 4**\n- Typically used for **multi-GPU configurations** or specific parallel scheduling scenarios\n\n## Key Context\n\nHere's the honest caveat: **the exact behavioral differences between these modes are not well-documented** in public Intel documentation. The `ccs_mode` parameter is a kernel module parameter for the `xe` driver, and the precise semantics of each numeric value aren't officially published in detail.\n\n## Practical Guidance\n\n- **For most single-GPU users:** Stick with the default (auto/0). The differences are subtle and rarely noticeable in everyday use.\n- **For tuning:** If you're experimenting, the typical approach is to test with your specific workload (gaming, compute, etc.) and benchmark.\n- **Mode 4:** This is where I'd be most cautious\u2014multi-GPU or specialized scheduling. If you're running a single A770, you almost certainly don't need it.\n\n## How to Set It\n\n```bash\n# Check current setting\ncat /sys/module/xe/parameters/ccs_mode\n\n# Set via kernel command line (grub) or modprobe config\noptions xe c"}